**Lemma 5.4 (actual-image identification with the activation choice).**
On $\Omega$, $K_+(h)=K_+(k)$ implies $h=k$. Consequently $Y(h)$ and
the complete joint endpoint each determine the actual history, including
its original and auxiliary occurrence identities.

Proof. Apply AIF's literal replay algorithm with two additional explicit
interfaces. It starts at the same primitive foundation and uses the
recovered seed to reconstruct the unique padded packet and original paid
boot/acquisition. Feed $J_0$ only at old variable-frame INs. If $A\ne\bot$,
select the separately recorded Install at the old Ready after exactly
$A$ complete frames, before ordinary $D_0$ row lookup there. Otherwise
never choose Install. Follow the actual canonical $E(L)$ bootstrap,
fixed capture frame, $E(D_1)$ bootstrap and initialization rows. Feed
$J_1$ only at subsequent external frame INs. No other row consumes these
variable reservoirs. Stop after exactly the supplied $n$ native events.

This is the supplier's finite-prefix deterministic replay, with its
activation and input selectors now fixed by Lemma 5.3. The usual induction
has no missing choice: at every non-IN, other than that one recorded
instrument selection, the prescribed row is fixed; each code IN is the
prescribed public compilation bit; each variable IN is the next bit of
its matching retained reservoir. Original private preparation is fixed
by $u,H$. Equal pre-row raw fields yield the same reads, gates, writes,
marks, constructors, tokens, descriptor origins and receipt. In particular
an earlier Call is simulated through its actual native rows, not answered
by a recursive semantic oracle.

The induction covers every interior row of installation, banks, partial
fields, raw source overwrite, rejects, both copies and return. Actual-image
membership ensures that the finite reservoirs suffice up to $n$; replay
does not consume a future bit. Thus the algorithm identifies exactly $h$,
so equal keys identify the same prefix. $Y$ contains the selected material
of $M$, and a complete endpoint contains $Y$. Exact native occurrence
names follow the deterministic constructors; fixed template roles give
the structural auxiliary names. This does not authenticate an arbitrary
submitted key or acquire anything on behalf of a receiver. ∎

